rse you know one  of the touchstones is apollo 13. but a  lot of it is that he wanted authenticity  and realism because these things happen  and we had a lot of access and it was  incredible because you know in our  production you know place in australia  there were just wall to wall  basically like details and specs and  designs and every little detail from the  costumes to the colors of things that  are basically underground  was like you know we really considered  every little moment of that and i think  that that was you know it was apparent  since day one when you know ron was  asking me to have a look at the script  that he really wanted something that as  he said that if a thai person watches it  that they may not even know necessarily  if it's actually like you know like done  by a typers or not because the accuracy  is incredibly high and you know that's  one of the reasons why you know ron  ashley asked me to be part of it and you  know i i hope i did an okay job  we're  we're getting good results thank you but  uh you know what  i've learned in in recent years  especially doing movies about real  events that um  if you as a production team can really  immerse yourself in the subject ahead of  time  that it just on some in sort of  intrinsic intuitive level you you you  you create something that's a little  more engrossing for the audience it's  just more detailed audiences are just  more and more sophisticated by the by  the week  and um and it's the reason why we had to  have so much thai language in the movie  because  i would demand that of a film that i was  seeing based on some true event like  this but as raymond was saying we we  have i what i like to do is put video  monitors up in the production uh  department uh and over by the food  because people tend to head over to  where to the kitchen uh and in various  other places and have on the loop you  know all the news footage i can find all  the documentaries that i can find all  the reef all the all the all the photo  resources up on the walls so that you're  as you're prepping the movie you  actually can stop and and look at an  image or see a scene unfold in on it  from uh you know from from the  television monitor and just say how does  that relate to this scene that we're  going to try to recreate and it just  brings so much more sort of authority to  people's  understanding of what the what the truth  is and the honesty around around the the  subject i will admit that um when i  first heard about what happened i was  amazed that the kids were able to swim  out after all of that you know after  what happened and it was only watching  the documentary that i learned i don't  want to say it for people that are going  to  thank you  watch the movie  but what a twist  yeah it was i had no idea  well it's a you know it's amazing and  there's and look there are all kinds of  acts of courage that are demonstrated in  the real event  and you know i selected a few to try to  underscore it's an amazing story this is  a story that just goes on and on and on  and and and you can keep looking at it  from different vantages and and it's all  it's all valid and i you know if people  are intrigued by this  i certainly hope they see our film uh  but you know our film is going to be  seen slightly through a different lens  than the documentaries anything else you  might see down the road that's going to  be its own perspective as well all all  all valid and all really interesting but  it there are there are so many instances  of people making difficult decisions  making sacrifices that takes courage  and that's heroic and the outcome was uh  gratefully uh something that everyone  can be proud of for the rest of their  lives uh something that i thought you  guys did a really good job with uh one  of the many things is the water  sequences were very claustrophobic  and obviously you need to really put the  act like basically can you talk a little  bit about filming the water